From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Utkarsh Dalal <utkarsh.dalal@toptal.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 16:06:54 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Windows: honor ExtendedVolatileMetadata ranges and
 whole-module entries

Since FEX-2608 a block is relaxed by volatile metadata only when a listed
instruction lies inside it. That is right for PE volatile metadata, where
the listed instructions are what the compiler marked, but it leaves a
FEX_EXTENDEDVOLATILEMETADATA range or whole-module entry without effect:
those entries list no instructions.

Keep the ranges that come from the option in their own list. A block that
lies entirely inside one is translated without TSO for its integer loads
and stores; listed instructions and the Unity displacement accesses keep
TSO, and plain loads before them are fenced as in PE metadata blocks. A
block that crosses a range boundary keeps TSO. Modules that only carry PE
volatile metadata behave as before. The Linux frontend is unchanged.

An entry can name a module by its image ID,
`<lowercase file name>-<XXH3 of the name ^ (SizeOfImage << 32 | TimeDateStamp)>`,
which upstream already accepts as a key. Such an entry applies only to the
build it was written for. When another build of that module loads, the
entry is ignored and one info line names both IDs, so a game update that
ships a different DLL under the same name keeps full TSO.
---
